From udd632bd3bbcdd7e9 at groups.google.com  Sat Aug 31 13:39:43 2019
From: udd632bd3bbcdd7e9 at groups.google.com (Perry de Valpine)
Date: Sat, 31 Aug 2019 13:39:43 +0000
Subject: Re: Simple Code Runs in JAGS but not in Nimble
Message-ID: <GhRLyxqQYGI.skXFwI-7BgAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <GhRLyxqQYGI.OIH-Lm-yBgAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
References: <GhRLyxqQYGI.M7ARoTNoBgAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com> <GhRLyxqQYGI.OIH-Lm-yBgAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/GhRLyxqQYGI/m/skXFwI-7BgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It looks like you want Q*u[i] to be the standard deviation.  In JAGS, you create tau = precision = 1/(Q*u[i])^2.  In nimble it looks like you are using Q*u[i] and expecting it to be standard deviation.

nimble uses the same default parameterization as BUGS/JAGS, not R.  It does extend BUGS/JAGS by supporting alternative parameterizations via named parameters. Therefore the models you've used are not the same.  In nimble you are saying precision = Q*u[i].  It looks like what you intend is

M[i] ~ dnorm(mu[i], sd = Q*u[i])

Distributions and parameterizations are described in the nimble User Manual available at r-nimble.org <http://r-nimble.org>.

-Perry

